{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>She shall remain</b> — תֵּשֵׁב means not sitting but tarrying, as in \"וַתֵּשְׁבוּ בְקָדֵשׁ\" (Deuteronomy 1:46), \"וַיֵּשֶׁב בְּאֵלוֹנֵי מַמְרֵא\" (Genesis 13:18) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 12:4). If so, the verse says: for thirty-three days she shall further wait, so that she shall not touch any consecrated thing and shall not come to the Sanctuary, even though these are days of purity regarding her husband — and this is the reason for \"בִּדְמֵי טָהֳרָה\" (during the blood of purification). And it spoke in this manner to inform that even if she does not see [flow] during these days, she must still wait on their account because of the birth. And it is possible that the meaning of \"תֵּשֵׁב\" here is like \"יָמִים רַבִּים תֵּשְׁבִי לִי לֹא תִזְנִי וְלֹא תִהְיִי לְאִישׁ\" (Hosea 3:3), for a woman who lies with her husband is called one who \"sits\" with him. And since regarding the seven [days] it said \"וְטָמְאָה שִׁבְעַת יָמִים כִּימֵי נִדַּת דְּוֹתָהּ תִּטְמָא\" — that she is impure to her husband and to consecrated things for all seven [days] — it then said that after the seven she may \"sit\" with her husband for thirty-three days during the blood of purification, but she shall not touch consecrated things and shall not come to the Sanctuary even if she does not see [flow], and she may sit with her husband even if she does see [flow].\n\nAnd what seems correct in my eyes is that a woman during the days of her flow is called \"נִדָּה\" (נִדָּה, banished one) because people banish her and distance her — all people, both men and women, keep away from her, and she sits alone, not conversing with people at all, for even her speech is impure to them, and the dust she treads upon is impure to them like the dust of a corpse's decomposed bones. Our Sages also mentioned this, and even her gaze causes harm — and I already recalled this in the portion of Vayetze Jacob (Genesis 31:35). The practice of menstruants was to dwell in a special tent, as Rachel said to her father (ibid.): \"כִּי לֹא אוּכַל לָקוּם מִפָּנֶיךָ כִּי דֶרֶךְ נָשִׁים לִי\" — for their custom was that she would not walk nor set the sole of her foot upon the ground. Therefore the Torah was stringent regarding the sitting and lying down of a menstruant more than regarding contact. So too Scripture said regarding the metzora (Leviticus 13:46): \"בָּדָד יֵשֵׁב מִחוּץ לַמַּחֲנֶה מוֹשָׁבוֹ\" — and it did not say, as it said regarding other impure persons (Deuteronomy 23:11): \"וְיָצָא אֶל מִחוּץ לַמַּחֲנֶה לֹא יָבֹא אֶל תּוֹךְ הַמַּחֲנֶה\" — for there it mentioned sitting, meaning he shall not walk about at all, for his odor and his breath cause harm. Therefore Scripture said here that for thirty-three days as well she shall sit during the blood of purification in her dwelling-place, just as she sat during the days of the birth-impurity, and it warned by way of prohibition that she shall not touch consecrated things and shall not come to the Sanctuary.\n\nAnd its midrash (Sifra here): \"תֵּשֵׁב\" — to bring about the stringency that even on the eleventh [day] she is pure from ziva-flow. One might think she is pure from the status of niddah as well — Scripture teaches: \"דְּוֹתָהּ תִּטְמָא\"." "And the reason for <b>בִּדְמֵי טָהֳרָה</b> according to Rashi's view is to say that even if she saw [flow] she is pure by Torah law — and thus said Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 12:4) — for the reason is that it is blood of purity as opposed to the blood of niddah and does not impurify, and HaShem decreed regarding a male [child] a number of days corresponding to the time his form is completed in the womb, and regarding a female double that — and this is a clear and tested matter. According to my opinion, the meaning of טָהֳרָה is cleansing, as in \"זָהָב טָהוֹר\" (Exodus 25:39) — refined and purged — and similarly \"וְיָשַׁב מְצָרֵף וּמְטַהֵר כֶּסֶף וְטִהַר אֶת בְּנֵי לֵוִי וְזִקַּק אֹתָם\" (Malachi 3:3). The concept is that He commanded regarding one who gives birth to a male that she be impure for seven days, as during her menstrual impurity, for it is usual that blood flows copiously from the source, and He commanded that she then continue another thirty-three days, sitting in her house to cleanse her body, for during all these [days] she expels the dregs of the blood and the turbid, putrefied moisture that comes from the extract of the blood — and then she is cleansed from the birth, from the womb, and from pregnancy, and may come to the House of YHWH. Our Sages received that she is pure to her husband during these days, because regarding the seven it said that they are like the days of her menstrual impurity, but regarding these it said that she is impure to consecrated things and to the Sanctuary — not to ordinary things and not to her husband, as they said (Ḥullin 31a): her husband is ordinary.\n\nAnd the reason for the doubling in the case of a female: either according to the words of Rabbi Abraham, following the view of Rabbi Ishmael, who said that the male is completed at forty-one days and the female at eighty-one (Niddah 30a); but according to the Sages, who said that both male and female are completed at forty-one [days], the reason is because the nature of the female is cold and moist, and the whiteness in the mother's womb is very abundant and cold, and therefore she gave birth to a female — and therefore she needs greater cleansing because of the abundance of moisture and putrefied blood in them, and because of coldness, as is well known that cold organs require more time for their cleansing than warm ones."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The midrashic objection and resolution at the end refers to the halakhic question of whether the eleventh day after the seven days of niddah-impurity counts toward purification from ziva (gonorrheal) impurity; the answer is that she remains impure from niddah but pure from ziva."}